1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,217
In the last class, you moved a crew's standing in one sitting.

2
00:00:07,017 --> 00:00:09,191
A campaign asks something harder.

3
00:00:10,091 --> 00:00:12,265
How fast does it move,

4
00:00:12,715 --> 00:00:16,628
and what does it open when it gets there?

5
00:00:18,428 --> 00:00:22,776
Today you'll build a door that only trust can open.

6
00:00:23,676 --> 00:00:28,893
And then a way for the crew to slam it on themselves.

7
00:00:30,093 --> 00:00:33,137
First, what actually moves standing over weeks.

8
00:00:33,587 --> 00:00:35,326
I measured every row.

9
00:00:37,126 --> 00:00:38,865
Finishing a side's job.

10
00:00:39,315 --> 00:00:42,793
Five points, or ten for the harder kind.

11
00:00:43,693 --> 00:00:46,302
Paying the levy in a hail.

12
00:00:46,752 --> 00:00:49,361
Twenty points, for a hundred credits.

13
00:00:51,161 --> 00:00:54,204
And now look at the last row.

14
00:00:55,104 --> 00:00:58,583
Finishing a step of the story does nothing.

15
00:01:00,383 --> 00:01:02,557
You can write the words.

16
00:01:03,007 --> 00:01:06,050
Lint says clean, the credits are paid,

17
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:10,413
and the standing goes somewhere no door ever reads.

18
00:01:11,613 --> 00:01:16,830
That has a consequence, and it's the whole craft of this lecture.

19
00:01:18,630 --> 00:01:22,109
Nothing the crew does once can move standing.

20
00:01:23,009 --> 00:01:25,183
Jobs can be taken again.

21
00:01:25,633 --> 00:01:27,807
Answers can be given again.

22
00:01:29,607 --> 00:01:33,520
So you can't hand standing out as a prize.

23
00:01:34,420 --> 00:01:37,898
You can only put a price on it,

24
00:01:38,348 --> 00:01:40,522
in credits or in time.

25
00:01:41,422 --> 00:01:45,770
And you decide how much of each an evening pays.

26
00:01:46,970 --> 00:01:50,013
So here's a door, in three parts.

27
00:01:51,813 --> 00:01:56,596
A lead the crew can see from the very first evening.

28
00:01:57,046 --> 00:01:59,654
It tells them what opens it.

29
00:02:00,554 --> 00:02:04,033
That matters, because a door nobody knows about

30
00:02:04,483 --> 00:02:07,091
isn't a reason to do anything.

31
00:02:08,891 --> 00:02:11,935
Then one answer in the Compact's hail.

32
00:02:12,835 --> 00:02:17,617
After the key it says, if standing is forty or more.

33
00:02:18,067 --> 00:02:20,676
Below that, the button isn't there.

34
00:02:22,476 --> 00:02:25,085
And the record it leads to,

35
00:02:25,535 --> 00:02:27,274
where they're finally told.

36
00:02:28,474 --> 00:02:31,517
Why forty, and not some other number?

37
00:02:33,317 --> 00:02:36,361
At twenty it opens after one levy,

38
00:02:36,811 --> 00:02:40,724
on the first evening, and that isn't a door.

39
00:02:41,624 --> 00:02:45,102
At sixty, a crew may never get there.

40
00:02:46,902 --> 00:02:51,250
At forty, a crew that takes one job a week

41
00:02:51,700 --> 00:02:53,874
and pays the levy once

42
00:02:54,324 --> 00:02:56,933
arrives in about the fourth week.

43
00:02:57,833 --> 00:03:01,746
And that's where I want them to hear it,

44
00:03:02,196 --> 00:03:04,370
right before the act ends.

45
00:03:06,170 --> 00:03:10,083
So you choose the number by choosing the week.

46
00:03:11,283 --> 00:03:14,761
A door that only opens is a reward.

47
00:03:15,211 --> 00:03:18,689
A door that can close is a story.

48
00:03:20,489 --> 00:03:24,837
So in the Gleaners' hail, I add one tempting answer.

49
00:03:25,737 --> 00:03:28,780
Sell them a copy of the Count.

50
00:03:30,580 --> 00:03:32,754
It earns the Gleaners' respect,

51
00:03:33,204 --> 00:03:37,552
and in the same breath it costs the Compact's trust.

52
00:03:38,452 --> 00:03:43,235
An answer with one side can do a deed with another.

53
00:03:45,035 --> 00:03:46,774
Here's what I measured.

54
00:03:47,224 --> 00:03:49,833
Forty with the Compact became seventeen.

55
00:03:50,733 --> 00:03:53,341
And at home, three things changed.

56
00:03:54,241 --> 00:03:57,720
The greeting went cold, the manifest was gone,

57
00:03:58,170 --> 00:04:00,778
and so was the better work.

58
00:04:01,978 --> 00:04:03,717
Now you plan it.

59
00:04:04,617 --> 00:04:08,096
The ledger is one row for each evening,

60
00:04:08,546 --> 00:04:10,720
with the standing you expect,

61
00:04:11,170 --> 00:04:14,213
and the door you expect to open.

62
00:04:16,013 --> 00:04:20,796
The numbers are a guess, and the crew won't follow them.

63
00:04:21,696 --> 00:04:24,304
What matters is the last column.

64
00:04:26,104 --> 00:04:29,148
For each door, you've named an evening,

65
00:04:29,598 --> 00:04:33,511
and you can check the price against the wages.

66
00:04:34,411 --> 00:04:37,889
Too cheap, and it opens on night one.

67
00:04:38,339 --> 00:04:40,948
Too dear, and it never does.

68
00:04:42,748 --> 00:04:46,226
And remember where all of this is kept.

69
00:04:46,676 --> 00:04:50,589
It's in the ship's own record, in the save.

70
00:04:51,789 --> 00:04:54,398
Lint's clean, so here it is.

71
00:04:56,198 --> 00:04:58,372
Three answers, and no manifest.

72
00:04:59,272 --> 00:05:01,446
I pay the levy twice,

73
00:05:01,896 --> 00:05:04,070
and now there are four.

74
00:05:05,870 --> 00:05:08,913
I ask, and the lead is done.

75
00:05:10,713 --> 00:05:13,322
Then out to the breaking yard,

76
00:05:13,772 --> 00:05:15,946
and I sell the Count.

77
00:05:16,846 --> 00:05:19,454
Nothing tells me what that cost.

78
00:05:21,254 --> 00:05:25,602
Back home, the good work has gone from the list.

79
00:05:26,502 --> 00:05:29,980
And the hail's down to three answers again.

80
00:05:31,180 --> 00:05:34,659
So you price standing, you don't award it.

81
00:05:36,459 --> 00:05:39,937
Every answer that earns it gets a cost.

82
00:05:40,837 --> 00:05:44,750
And all of it lives in the ship's record,

83
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:48,678
which the save carries from week to week.

84
00:05:50,478 --> 00:05:56,565
Next time, we look at the act from the crew's side of the table.
